From: Yusuf İhsan KILINÇ
Subject: [PATCH 0100] anv: AV1 video sessions hand out their internal buffers in a few memory bindings

On top of 0099 (src/mesa-vb1 = mesa-fd2 + this, build-vb1). NOT installed.

vkGetVideoSessionMemoryRequirementsKHR reported one binding per internal buffer: an Xe2 AV1 encode session with HuC
BRC had 57, an Xe2 AV1 film-grain decode session 41 (upstream ANV reports the same 41 on Xe2: its enum has 41 AV1
entries and none is skipped there). The Vulkan Video samples, and the CTS AV1 encoder built on them, read the
requirements into a fixed array of 40 (VulkanVideoSession.cpp, MAX_BOUND_MEMORY): every AV1 encode case overflowed
the stack (SIGSEGV / __stack_chk_fail), the CTS decode path refused film grain with InternalError.

AV1 sessions now group their buffers into memory bindings (memoryBindIndex 0..n-1): the AVP line/tile buffers, the
default CDF tables (CPU-written), DBD + tile size/bitstream streamout, the Xe2 VDEnc row stores + statistics, and the
HuC BRC buffers. Decode: 3 bindings, encode: 5 (4 without BRC). Inside a binding each buffer sits at its own
alignment (4096) in enum order, with its old size; the binding's memoryTypeBits are the buffers' (all identical, the
xemac CPU-visible VRAM selection of 0011 included) and its alignment their maximum. vkBindVideoSessionMemoryKHR fans
a binding out into vid->vid_mem[type] (memory, memoryOffset + sub-offset, size), so command emission, CPU
initialisation and queries keep addressing vid_mem[type] unchanged. One layout function answers both entry points.
H.264, H.265 and VP9 keep one binding per buffer with the same memoryBindIndex values and sizes as before.
The requirements query now returns VK_INCOMPLETE when the array is short (spec; it returned VK_SUCCESS).

Validation (Mac, fake device with VCS/VECS + HuC, scratch instrumented builds of fd2 and this):
- bindings AV1 decode 39/41 -> 3, AV1 encode 57 -> 5 (176x144 ... 7680x4320, 8/10-bit, film grain up to 16K);
  H.264/H.265/VP9 identical; every internal buffer keeps its size, lies 4K-aligned inside its binding, no overlap;
  bytes: at most the per-buffer 4K page rounding the separate bindings had (AV1 encode 9,448,320 vs 9,457,664).
- AV1 encode batches of 13 configurations (CQP/CBR/VBR, quality 0/1/2, 8/10-bit, up to 8K multi-tile): every
  differing dword is an address resolving to the same internal buffer + offset (or same app BO + offset); with the
  session memory in one allocation the batches are byte-identical.
- CTS AV1/H.264/H.265/VP9 decode and H.264/H.265 encode cases: same results; non-AV1 batches byte-identical, AV1
  equal modulo addresses. gv00-gv04 case by case identical; gv07 film grain InternalError 6 -> 0; gv13 AV1 encode
  CRASH 56 -> 0 (the encoder now runs; the fake device writes no bitstream).

Generated-by: LLM
